# driftwatch — build-context filter for deploy/container/Containerfile (docker/BuildKit).
#
# KEEP IN SYNC with Containerfile.containerignore, which is byte-identical apart from this
# header. Two files exist because the two runtimes look for two different names and neither
# can include the other; install.sh verifies both are present before it will build.
#
# The Containerfile's COPY allowlist keeps client data out of the image LAYERS. This file
# keeps it out of the build CONTEXT, which is a separate problem with the same consequence.
#
# The context is the repository ROOT, and `engagements/<id>/` lives there: vault, snapshots,
# findings, evidence, audit logs. Without this filter every build hands that whole tree to
# the builder — to a root-owned daemon under /var/lib/docker with docker, to a temporary
# copy with podman. Neither location is reachable by `driftwatch teardown`, which is exactly
# the argument design §15.4 makes about layers. Excluding it here is the other half of the
# same control, not a build-speed optimisation.
#
# Used explicitly, never by lucky default:
#   podman:  ./install.sh --mode container   ->  podman build --ignorefile <this file> ...
#   docker:  the identical sidecar Containerfile.dockerignore, with DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1
#            forced by install.sh (the legacy builder ignores sidecars and would sweep
#            engagements/ into the context).
#
# Structure mirrors the Containerfile and scope.yml: deny everything, then name what may
# enter. A path forgotten here fails the build with "COPY failed" — loud and fail-closed —
# rather than silently carrying a client's credentials into the builder.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- deny everything first
*

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- then the allowlist
# Exactly the sources the Containerfile COPYs, and nothing else. deploy/ is deliberately
# absent: the image does not ship the container tooling that builds it.
!requirements.txt
!requirements.yml
!ansible.cfg
!README.md
!bin
!scripts
!playbooks
!roles
!rules
!allowlists
!inventory
!systemd
!docs
!tests
!baselines
!vendor
!response

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- re-deny, last word wins
# The response layer is a separate privilege domain (design §7, §14): its CODE ships, its
# live cases and evidence never do. These lines come after the allowlist because the last
# matching pattern wins.
response/cases
response/evidence

# Belt and braces. None of these are re-included above, so they are already excluded by the
# leading `*`; they are named so that loosening the allowlist later cannot silently pull a
# client's data or the operator's keys into a build.
engagements
reports
snapshots
.git
.venv
collections
**/vault
**/vault.yml
**/*.vault
**/vault_pass*
**/krb5.conf
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/id_rsa*

# Build noise: bytecode caches and test/lint state are not inputs to anything.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
